<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chen dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">557 CE – 589 CE · capital Jiankang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last of the Southern Dynasties, ruling the lower Yangtze from Jiankang until the Sui conquest of 589 reunited China after nearly three centuries of division.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-dynasty">Liang dynasty</a> collapsed in the 550s under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a> rebellion and northern invasions. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-chen">Chen Baxian</a>, who had defeated Hou Jing, gained control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> and in 557 accepted the abdication of the boy Emperor Jing of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a>, founding Chen with himself as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chen was the smallest of the southern states, having lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and the lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, and it relied on local strongmen as much as on the old émigré aristocracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> remained a centre of Buddhist learning and courtly poetry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuan-of-chen">Emperor Xuan</a> recovered the Huai river region in 573 but lost it again to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> by 579.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-shubao">Chen Shubao</a>, became a byword for frivolous palace life. In 589 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> armies crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> with little resistance; Chen Shubao was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and lived out his life in comfortable captivity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was united again under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chen-dynasty.jpg" alt="Chen dynasty" width="320" height="333"><figcaption>Image: 长官司 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S-Chen_576.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>557 CE – 589 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jiankang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chen Baxian (Emperor Wu), Chen Xu (Emperor Xuan), Chen Shubao</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=573&amp;focus=chen-dynasty" aria-label="Chen dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7737 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chen dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8057 1267L8057 1267M8057 1267L7769 1191"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8057 1267h0M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8057 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8057 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jiankang. <a href="/atlas?year=573&amp;focus=chen-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
